The quoted code exists verbatim, unguarded, at the cited location.

/Users/labeaaa/Developer/ditto-edge-studio/SwiftUI/EdgeStudio/Data/LogFileParser.swift:218-235
```swift
private static func decompressGzip(_ data: Data) -> Data? {
    // Skip the 10-byte gzip header + 8-byte trailer and decompress the payload
    guard data.count > 18 else { return nil }
    ...
    // Use original size hint from gzip trailer (last 4 bytes = uncompressed size mod 2^32)
    let sizeBytes = data.subdata(in: (data.count - 4) ..< data.count)
    let originalSize = sizeBytes.withUnsafeBytes { ptr in
        ptr.load(as: UInt32.self).littleEndian
    }

    // Allocate output buffer — use 4x as fallback if size hint is 0
    let bufferSize = originalSize > 0 ? Int(originalSize) : compressedPayload.count * 8
    var outputBuffer = [UInt8](repeating: 0, count: bufferSize)
```
`guard data.count > 18` is the only validation in the function. There is no upper clamp on `originalSize`, no cross-check against `data.count`, and no gzip-magic check. `grep -n "1f8b\|0x1f\|magic\|fileSize\|resourceValues\|maxSize\|clamp\|min(" LogFileParser.swift DittoLogCaptureService.swift` returns **zero matches** — no guard exists anywhere on this path.

